  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1224 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nFor the weekend into early next week, the models generally agree in \nshowing mid and upper level high pressure moving from the desert \nsouthwest into the southern U.S. plains and eventually setting up \nover the southeastern U.S. There will be a mid-level weakness over \nthe northwestern Gulf, but no significant weather is expected with \nthis feature. We still expect to see an increase in the low-level \nthermal ridge, which will likely yield a degree or two upward trend \nin temperatures across the region. We will continue to monitor for \nthe possibility of maximum temperatures and peak heat index values \nnear Heat Advisory criteria for areas generally along and east of \nInterstate 35 for the upcoming weekend and possibly into Monday. \nMost areas will remain dry, but some isolated seabreeze convection \nremains possible near the coastal plains.  \n\nThe area of mid and upper level high pressure over the southeastern \nU.S. will allow for a deeper south to southeasterly flow in the \nlower and mid-levels of the atmosphere to develop. No significant \nchange in temperatures is anticipated at this time with above normal \nwarmth expected to continue into the middle of the upcoming week.",
